{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 310,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That Einstein guy was a real bummer for our hopes of a star-hopping, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4230,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "science-fiction-y future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5230,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "His whole “nothing travels faster than light” rule seems to ensure that exploration of even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9440,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the local part of our galaxy will be an excruciating slow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12410,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Einstein also gave us a glimmer of hope."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15050,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He showed us that space and time can be warped - and so the warp drive was conceived."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20490,
    "dDurationMs": 5102,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just recently, a couple of papers contend\nthat this is not pure science fiction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30380,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1915, Albert Einstein’s general theory\nof relativity revealed that the fabric of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34150,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space and time is mutable and dynamic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37400,
    "dDurationMs": 1450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can, in fact, be warped."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38850,
    "dDurationMs": 4101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can’t travel through space at faster\nthan the speed of light - but there’s no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42951,
    "dDurationMs": 2739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed limit for the fabric of space itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45690,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This hints at a possibility for faster than\nlight, or superluminal travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50350,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And only 15 years after general relativity’s\ndebut, the warp drive was invented, propelling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55950,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "humanity on its first journeys to the stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58910,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That “invention” and those journeys were\nin the 1931 spring edition of Amazing Stories."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65430,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Islands of Space, its authorJohn Cambell talks about increasing the cosmic speed limit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70970,
    "dDurationMs": 2369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by curving the fabric of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73339,
    "dDurationMs": 2121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea caught on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75460,
    "dDurationMs": 4279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to the wonderful historical dictionary of science fiction, the first mention of a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79739,
    "dDurationMs": 6681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“space-warp” as a propulsion mechanism\nwas in J. Williamson’s 1936 story The Cometeers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86420,
    "dDurationMs": 5379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space-warp drives, time-warp drives, and eventually just warp drives became an increasingly popular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91799,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way of breaking Einstein’s rules."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93559,
    "dDurationMs": 4801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The pretend technology really took off with\nStar Trek in the 60s - and Star Trek inspired"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98360,
    "dDurationMs": 5229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the very first real warp field solution to\nthe Einstein field equations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103589,
    "dDurationMs": 5951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s the Alcubierre warp field, derived\nby Mexican physicist and star trek aficionado"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109540,
    "dDurationMs": 1490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Miguel Alcubierre."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111030,
    "dDurationMs": 2879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, we covered Alcubierre’s idea 5 years\nago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113909,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Back then the warp field solution looked like a very rigorous, carefully calculated work"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118019,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of pure fiction - it broke the laws of physics in ways that I’ll come back to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123159,
    "dDurationMs": 4459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Subsequent studies have tried to fix this. And a very recent paper claims to "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127618,
    "dDurationMs": 4022,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have solved the worst deal breaker in Alcubierre’s proposal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131640,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to pull them apart two new papers on warp drives and see if it’s really time to don our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137310,
    "dDurationMs": 3089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Star Trek cosplay and warp out of here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140399,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get to the new work, let’s talk\nlight speed and warp drives."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145149,
    "dDurationMs": 4361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein’s speed limit doesn’t directly\nsay that nothing can travel faster than light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149510,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "His special theory of relativity just says\nthat it takes infinite energy to accelerate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153940,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anything with positive mass all the way to\nlight speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157710,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That effectively means that it’s impossible\nto observe a massive object cross this speed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163260,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "barrier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164260,
    "dDurationMs": 1479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are loopholes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165739,
    "dDurationMs": 5171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In certain circumstances we can think of space itself as moving - and there’s no limit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170910,
    "dDurationMs": 6079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the relative motion of two patches of space - and so objects in those patches could have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176989,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superluminal speeds relative to each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180349,
    "dDurationMs": 5151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example inside black holes where we can think of space as flowing downwards faster"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185500,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186520,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or beyond the cosmic horizon, the expanding universe is carrying galaxies away from us"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191780,
    "dDurationMs": 1240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "faster than light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193020,
    "dDurationMs": 4829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The warp drive takes advantage of this loophole by accelerating a patch of space relative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197849,
    "dDurationMs": 1811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to its surroundings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199660,
    "dDurationMs": 5079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Objects in that warp bubble move with that\npatch without themselves ever feeling any"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204739,
    "dDurationMs": 1011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acceleration."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205750,
    "dDurationMs": 3129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is what the Alcubierre drive is supposed\nto do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208879,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a sp acetime geometry that is a valid\nsolution to the equations general theory of relativity. The Einstein Field Equation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216879,
    "dDurationMs": 5151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It includes a comfortable, flat region of space surrounded by a region of extreme spatial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222030,
    "dDurationMs": 1240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "curvature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223270,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The space behind this bubble is expanded,\nwhile the space in front is contracted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228570,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The resulting push-pull propels the bubble\nand any spaceship that it contains."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233470,
    "dDurationMs": 4519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Alcubierre warp field may be a valid solution to the Einstein field equations,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237989,
    "dDurationMs": 2431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but that doesn’t mean it’s physically\npossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240420,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Normally, to solve these equations we input a physically possible distribution of mass,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245860,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy, on the right side and it spits\nout the spacetime geometry on the left."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250640,
    "dDurationMs": 6019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alcubierre instead solved the equations backwards - he figured out the geometry of a warp bubble,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256659,
    "dDurationMs": 4361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then ran it backwards through the equations to see what distribution of matter and energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261020,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be required."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262350,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can do this with any geometry, but almost every geometry will produce an impossible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268360,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass-energy distribution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270770,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To combat this, we have a set of energy conditions that go alongside the Einstein field equations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276260,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that are meant to restrict the allowable energy distributions to what is physically possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281600,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alcubierre’s field breaks all of these energy conditions, and he’s upfront about that fact."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288070,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can summarize this by saying that it requires a negative energy density, which should be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295190,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible except perhaps on the tiniest, quantum scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299320,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sometimes people talk about the drive requiring negative mass - also called exotic matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304540,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and yeah, that would do the trick too - and is also probably impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308800,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that sounds like a dealbreaker."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311300,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other minor hiccup is that Alcubierre’s\noriginal field required more energy than is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315570,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contained in all the matter in the visible\nuniverse to move a moderate-sized starship."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321290,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Subsequent studies improved on Alcubierre’s warp design and brought down the energy requirement"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326210,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to less insane levels - although they typically remained somewhat insane."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330200,
    "dDurationMs": 5979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the requirement of exotic matter didn’t\ngo away, and in fact subsequent studies demonstrated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336179,
    "dDurationMs": 4551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that any superluminal warp drive MUST use negative energy densities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340730,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Other studies discovered new problems - for example, warp bubbles are un-steerable because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345800,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the interior of the bubble is causally disconnected from parts of the front warp field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351770,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That inspired Sergey Krasnikov to come up\nwith his Krasnikov tube - a sort of warp conveyor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "belt, which has the slightly useless property of only working if it’s a full return journey"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363640,
    "dDurationMs": 2539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and you don’t get off at the destination."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366179,
    "dDurationMs": 4721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To me, one of the biggest deal-breakers is\nthat any faster-than-light travel can be used"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370900,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to create closed time like curves, as we discussed previously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375350,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That leads to paradoxes - to contradictions and inconsistencies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378850,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the appearance of inconsistencies is one of our most powerful indications that our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384480,
    "dDurationMs": 3070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theoretical musings are on the wrong track."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387550,
    "dDurationMs": 6909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, yeah, warp fields became much better studied, but remained implausible - and probably impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394459,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I guess the dreams of wannabe starship captains are strong - because the work continued,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400999,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and just recently two papers have claimed\nbreakthroughs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404119,
    "dDurationMs": 6491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll start with the paper entitled Introducing Physical Warp Drives by Alexey Bobrick and Gianni Martire."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410610,
    "dDurationMs": 4919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These guys propose a general definition for warp fields not tied to a particular solution"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415529,
    "dDurationMs": 2011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the Einstein field equations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417540,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To quote the authors: Warp drives are inertially moving shells of positive or negative energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423300,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "material which enclose a `passenger' region with a flat metric."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428050,
    "dDurationMs": 2669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s focus on that “inertially moving”\npart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430719,
    "dDurationMs": 5321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bobrick and Martire is that no past warp solution has in-built mode of acceleration."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436040,
    "dDurationMs": 5459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of them, including Alcubierre’s, move\nwith whatever velocity they started at."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441499,
    "dDurationMs": 6761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This isn’t exactly a new insight - in 2002,\nJose Natario demonstrated that the whole expansion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448260,
    "dDurationMs": 6341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and contraction of space was only a side-effect of Alcubierre’s choice of warp field, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454601,
    "dDurationMs": 3349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he constructed a warp field without that feature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457950,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Natario, and now Bobrick and Martire, define warp fields as bubbles that slide through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463510,
    "dDurationMs": 3449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space - potentially at superluminal speeds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466959,
    "dDurationMs": 5731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But all these guys agree that superluminal\nbubbles are only possible if the warp field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472690,
    "dDurationMs": 2450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uses exotic matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475140,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bobrick and Martire also point out that there’s still no way to accelerate a warp bubble"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479730,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across the light speed limit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481850,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A superluminal warp bubble has to have started out superluminal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486200,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This seems in conflict with Alcubierre himself, who wrote that the warp bubble “pushes” the spaceship"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492020,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- accelerates it from rest."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494120,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this acceleration isn’t actually derived\nfrom his warp field solution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499670,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The velocity of the bubble is baked into the\nequation for the field geometry, but it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503830,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not clear how you change that velocity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506670,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This isn’t necessarily a complete downer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508070,
    "dDurationMs": 5489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As pointed out by Fransisco Lobo and Matt\nVisser, if sub-light-speed or subluminal warp"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513559,
    "dDurationMs": 5411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubbles can be a thing, these might be an example of a “reaction-less” drive - a propulsion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518970,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "method that doesn’t need any propellant\n- not stuff ejected from behind like in a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522940,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rocket."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523940,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these researchers also note that even\na subluminal reaction-less warp drive would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528470,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still require negative energy and an enormous amount of it for any decent sized spaceship."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534270,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, let’s move on to the second paper by\nErik Lentz: “Breaking the Warp Barrier:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540390,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hyper-Fast Solitons in Einstein-Maxwell-Plasma Theory”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544310,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This one is a little more star trek friendly\nthan, well, pretty much every prior effort."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550450,
    "dDurationMs": 5719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lentz claims to have found an actual superluminal warp field solution that does NOT require"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556169,
    "dDurationMs": 2631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the impossible negative energy densities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558800,
    "dDurationMs": 5359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He does this by exploring a broader family\nof solutions to the Einstein field equations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564159,
    "dDurationMs": 1781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than previous studies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565940,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The details are technical to put it mildly\n- but let me try to give you a sense of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571360,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572360,
    "dDurationMs": 4512,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can think of the warp field as a special type of isolated wave moving through space - "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576872,
    "dDurationMs": 1998,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what we call a soliton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578870,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In previous work, this wave only waved in\nthe direction of motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582740,
    "dDurationMs": 2169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That gave them an axial symmetry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584909,
    "dDurationMs": 6031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example in the Alcubierre field the warp is in front and behind the spaceship, while the exotic matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590940,
    "dDurationMs": 2890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is in a ring around the direction of motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593830,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you calculate the total energy with only this component than it’s alway negative."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600490,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lentz found that by including components of the wave motion that were perpendicular to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604780,
    "dDurationMs": 6620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the direction of motion he could build a superluminal soliton in which the energy came out positive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611400,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everywhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612400,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He came up with this example - here projected onto a 2-D plane."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618330,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Energy is distributed along these tracks, potentially as a plasma, in some places at some pretty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624140,
    "dDurationMs": 2230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "insane temperatures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626370,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This would resulting in expansion and contraction of space in similar regions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631270,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The entire patch of spacetime would travel at superluminal speeds, carrying a spaceship"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635810,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636810,
    "dDurationMs": 2350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A couple of minor issues persist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639160,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The energy required to carry a 100-meter diameter bubble is about the rest-mass energy of a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644740,
    "dDurationMs": 1650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tenth of our Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646390,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s hard to see how packing that much mass into these strips would NOT create a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652680,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, Lentz admits that this warp bubble is\ninertial just like Bobrick and Martire assert for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659520,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alcubierre’s bubble - it can exist at superluminal speeds in theory, but there’s no known way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664900,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get it to those speeds in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667180,
    "dDurationMs": 4299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both of these papers are peer reviewed and published in the reputable journal Classical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671479,
    "dDurationMs": 1551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Quantum Gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673030,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, that doesn’t mean they’re right - it just means that a couple of respectable physicists"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677090,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "refereed the works didn’t find any gigantic\nblunders - or did, and the blunders were fixed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682530,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But much more work is needed to verify anything this speculative."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687270,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can bet that there’ll be plenty of papers critiquing and/or supporting these efforts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691650,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So now you’re all up to date with warp technology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695530,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Superluminal warp fields exist in theory,\nand there’s a very tentative hints that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701200,
    "dDurationMs": 5269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worst deal-breaker can be resolved - that\nof requiring a non-existent type of energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706469,
    "dDurationMs": 5551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But other possibly-impossible hurdles remain - can the required energy densities be created"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712020,
    "dDurationMs": 4829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without creating black holes for any useful\nsized warp bubble?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716849,
    "dDurationMs": 5581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And can a warp bubble be created at sub-luminal speeds and then accelerated past the speed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722430,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of light?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723430,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So far there’s no known way to do this,\nand warp fields may suffer the same strict"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727760,
    "dDurationMs": 2029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed limit as does matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729789,
    "dDurationMs": 4511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And of course we still have the time travel\nissue - accelerating across the speed of light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734300,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "breaks causality and so probably can’t be\na thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737590,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein and the universe appear to be trolling us - alternately inspiring and crushing our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742070,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hopes for a star-hopping future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744260,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these studies have made one thing very clear - if warp travel is possible, humanity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749210,
    "dDurationMs": 2090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will figure it out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751300,
    "dDurationMs": 4169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Scientists are very persistent, especially the ones who are also die-hard science fiction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755469,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fans."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756469,
    "dDurationMs": 5581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’ll continue to try to “make it so”\nby exploring Einstein’s theory - hoping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762050,
    "dDurationMs": 4609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to build starship, but in the process learning how our universe works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766659,
    "dDurationMs": 6031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And possibly also building a starship, to propel humanity into the galaxy on waves of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772690,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "warped space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774840,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space Time gets an enormous amount of help\nfrom our Patreon supporters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777830,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You guys really help us plan ahead to more\nambitious episodes and series."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782090,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And today I want to give a special shoutout\nto Vinnie Falco, who is supporting us at the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786130,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Big Bang level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787130,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Vinnie, we’ve used your support to build\na very real warp drive, which, thanks to this new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792690,
    "dDurationMs": 3490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "result from Bobrick \u0026 Matire is now possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796180,
    "dDurationMs": 5289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These guys define a type-1 warp field as a\nsurface of positive energy density enclosing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801469,
    "dDurationMs": 1031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a flat metric."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802500,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the authors can correct me if I’m wrong,\nbut I think that this means literally any"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807000,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enclosed surface can make a warp field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809780,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like, for example, a cardboard box could be a warp drive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813690,
    "dDurationMs": 4099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So introducing the first type 1 warp drive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817789,
    "dDurationMs": 6571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re calling this version the Falco drive,\nand it’s guaranteed to be literally exactly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824360,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as effective as any warp drive ever constructed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828290,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Future versions will be even better."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830250,
    "dDurationMs": 4885,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We might add more tubes and some flashing lights for example."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835135,
    "dDurationMs": 4545,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, onto today’s comments we’re covering two episodes - there’s the quantum Zeno effect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839680,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the recent result from Fermilab’s muon\ng-2 experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843510,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll start with the muon g-2 result, which\nrevealed a possible a crack in the standard"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847740,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "model of particle physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849020,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A few of you asked whether similar experiments could be performed using the tau particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854390,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The tau is the heaviest of the 3 generations of leptons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857720,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The probability that a particle will interact\nwith other massive virtual particles is proportional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863250,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the square of that particle’s own mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865890,
    "dDurationMs": 5589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At nearly 17 times the mass of the muon, the tau should be even more sensitive to unknown"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871479,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872479,
    "dDurationMs": 5201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The problem is that the tau’s lifetime is\naround 10 million times shorter that the muon,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877680,
    "dDurationMs": 3359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which itself lives only for only microsecond\nor so."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881039,
    "dDurationMs": 6471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That makes it pretty hard to watch tau particles precess in magnetic fields, and so far the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887510,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "g-factor for the tau hasn’t even been measured to 1 significant figure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891700,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That said, there are other ways to track a\nparticle’s interactions with virtual particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896620,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the tau, the focus is in watching its\ndecay products."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900740,
    "dDurationMs": 5049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the energy distribution of those decays products are sensitive to the complex"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905789,
    "dDurationMs": 3791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interactions with virtual particles that happen during the decay."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909580,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "People aren’t really working on this, but\nyou can be sure that there’ll be even more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912380,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "focus on these experiments going forward."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914780,
    "dDurationMs": 5059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These could yield a truly independent verification\nof the muon g-2 result."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919839,
    "dDurationMs": 5901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Paul Vicory and Verdatum are impressed that Fermilab is producing cutting edge science"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925740,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even in competition with the much larger large hadron collider."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929810,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well not only has Fermilab remained competitive, it seems to have won the race - or at least"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935340,
    "dDurationMs": 4819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s ahead on the final stretch to find\nthat elusive crack in the standard model that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940159,
    "dDurationMs": 2271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may lead to whatever physics lies beyond."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942430,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is entirely due to Fermilab being\nextremely clever about where they focused"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948270,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their efforts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949270,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the LHC switched on and Fermilab’s\ntevatron accelerator was demoted from being"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954460,
    "dDurationMs": 4699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the largest in the world, Fermilab made the\nvery hard decision to switch the tevatron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959159,
    "dDurationMs": 4571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off and direct resources towards things like their neutrino experiments which we covered"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963730,
    "dDurationMs": 6970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "previously, and of course the muon g-2 - neither of which require quite such enormous accelerators."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970700,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fermilab’s scientists and engineers are\nright to feel very proud of this result."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974990,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, onto the quantum Zeno Effect, and the\nidea that you can freeze a quantum system"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979740,
    "dDurationMs": 1609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by observing it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981349,
    "dDurationMs": 5671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, the name comes from the Zeno paradox, which argues that a moving arrow isn’t moving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987020,
    "dDurationMs": 4319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it’s stationary at each separate\ninstant in time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991339,
    "dDurationMs": 4871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Raven Lord always thought that the idea of \"instantaneous velocity\" that solves this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996210,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paradox is just a theoretical artifact of\ncalculus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000130,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, in quantum mechanics, velocity isn’t just change in position with time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004820,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Momentum, which incorporates velocity, is\nas fundamental as position."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008440,
    "dDurationMs": 5599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So a particle at an instant in time really\ndoes have an instantaneous velocity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014039,
    "dDurationMs": 6531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For Flensdude the quantum Zeno effect reminds them of a poem composed by the Norse god Odin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020570,
    "dDurationMs": 3590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It goes like this:\nA fifth spell, I know; if I see shot with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024160,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ill intent; an arrow, travelling in a flight;\nit's incapable of flying too quickly; for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028870,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "me to stop it in its path; as long as I keep\nmy eyes on it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033410,
    "dDurationMs": 3230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we can conclude that Odin knew quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036640,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, stopping an arrow with the power of observation is a spell they teach you in undergrad"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042060,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043060,
    "dDurationMs": 3170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s also the first midterm exam, which\nreally helps cut down the class size."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046230,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, as everyone knows Odin plucked out his own eye in order to be able to drink from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050390,
    "dDurationMs": 1650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mythical well of knowledge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052040,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I thought this was just a fable teaching that “knowledge requiring sacrifice”, but now"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056090,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But now I see it’s an ancient viking metaphor for the Heisenberg uncertainty principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060460,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, don’t think I didn’t notice\nthat the last commenter - Raven Lord - bares"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065180,
    "dDurationMs": 1370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of the many names of the All-Father."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066550,
    "dDurationMs": 7330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So maybe Odin learned his quantum mechanics watching space time like the rest of us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073880,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alex Turner asks the real question: If the\nQuantum Zeno Effect plays a role in birds'"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1079460,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ability to see magnetic fields, then according to the Many-Worlds interpretation are there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084520,
    "dDurationMs": 3150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many, many worlds full of very, very lost birds?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087670,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I don’t think that’s quite how it works\n- instead there are a relatively very tiny"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1092970,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but non-zero number of worlds where the freezing of cryptochrome quantum states randomly failed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099250,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in all avian magnetoreception."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102800,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Given the extensive use of homing pigeons\nby the allied forces in WWII, my guess is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108460,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the Nazis would have won in all of these worlds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1110800,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hm, I think we just explained the premise of the Man in the High Castle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1116040,
    "dDurationMs": 750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nice one, physics."
    } ]
  } ]
}
